1
00:00:01,700 --> 00:00:09,580
Hello everyone! I'm a robot assistant of the Governance, Risk and Compliance team — GRC.

2
00:00:09,580 --> 00:00:15,670
Today I'll take you through Information Security Awareness Training.

3
00:00:15,670 --> 00:00:20,420
Our goal: protecting our digital workplace, together.

4
00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:27,020
Here is what we will cover. One: introduction and why it matters.

5
00:00:27,020 --> 00:00:30,110
Two: best practice for cyber security.

6
00:00:30,110 --> 00:00:35,000
Three: information classification. Four: social engineering.

7
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,670
Five: email and Telegram security guidelines.

8
00:00:38,670 --> 00:00:41,440
And finally, a summary and a quiz.

9
00:00:42,740 --> 00:00:47,130
What is information security awareness training?

10
00:00:47,130 --> 00:00:53,270
It's an educational program that helps every employee spot threats.

11
00:00:53,270 --> 00:00:57,660
Keep data secure. And build safe digital habits.

12
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:09,030
Why does it matter? One: it reduces human error and cyber attacks, helping employees avoid phishing, scams and security mistakes.

13
00:01:09,030 --> 00:01:15,750
Two: it builds a security culture, because cybersecurity is everyone's responsibility.

14
00:01:15,750 --> 00:01:21,840
Three: it protects our assets, preventing data leaks and keeping client trust.

15
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:26,760
Four: it meets compliance requirements such as ISO and PCI DSS.

16
00:01:28,210 --> 00:01:32,820
Let's start with best practice for cyber security.

17
00:01:32,820 --> 00:01:41,860
There are four common weak spots: password security, internet safety, clear desk and clear screen, and bring your own device — BYOD.

18
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:45,540
Most incidents start right here, with the basics.

19
00:01:46,840 --> 00:01:54,980
Number one: password security. Never share your password, change it regularly, and turn on two-factor authentication — 2FA.

20
00:01:54,980 --> 00:02:00,210
When you create a password, use at least 12 characters — the longer the better.

21
00:02:00,210 --> 00:02:04,310
Mix upper and lower case letters, numbers and special symbols.

22
00:02:04,310 --> 00:02:07,090
Use a different password for each account.

23
00:02:07,090 --> 00:02:11,720
And avoid personal information like birthdays, pet names or addresses.

24
00:02:13,020 --> 00:02:22,180
Look at the difference. A very weak password like qwerty or 123456 can be cracked in seconds to minutes.

25
00:02:22,180 --> 00:02:25,530
A good password takes months to years.

26
00:02:25,530 --> 00:02:33,980
A strong password can take centuries. The difference is just a little effort when you create it.

27
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:44,430
Number two: internet safety. On an HTTP website the data is not encrypted, so an attacker can read your password.

28
00:02:44,430 --> 00:02:49,780
HTTPS encrypts the data, so the attacker only sees scrambled text.

29
00:02:49,780 --> 00:02:53,430
Always make sure a website starts with https.

30
00:02:53,430 --> 00:03:03,550
Don't share sensitive information on public platforms, download files only from trusted sources, and don't click unknown ads.

31
00:03:03,550 --> 00:03:10,760
Remember: you have to be right all the time, but an attacker only needs to be right once.

32
00:03:12,210 --> 00:03:14,770
Number three: clear desk and clear screen.

33
00:03:14,770 --> 00:03:19,850
Lock your computer with Windows + L every time you leave your desk.

34
00:03:19,850 --> 00:03:24,220
Keep sensitive files and USB drives in locked drawers or cabinets.

35
00:03:24,220 --> 00:03:27,890
Erase whiteboards and clear your notes after meetings.

36
00:03:27,890 --> 00:03:29,710
Pick up printed documents immediately.

37
00:03:29,710 --> 00:03:33,380
And never write passwords on paper or sticky notes!

38
00:03:34,680 --> 00:03:41,670
Number four: bring your own device. Install the required firewall, antivirus or EDR.

39
00:03:41,670 --> 00:03:45,080
Update the OS and applications regularly.

40
00:03:45,080 --> 00:03:49,740
MFA is always required when accessing company resources.

41
00:03:49,740 --> 00:03:58,720
Encrypt data at rest and in transit. And if a device is lost or stolen, contact the IT Helpdesk immediately.

42
00:04:00,020 --> 00:04:08,620
Next, information classification. Information is classified into four levels, with sensitivity increasing from low to critical.

43
00:04:08,620 --> 00:04:13,910
Public: product promotions and news. Internal: newsletters and form templates.

44
00:04:13,910 --> 00:04:18,650
Confidential: medical information, department data and staff salaries.

45
00:04:18,650 --> 00:04:24,340
And Restricted, the most sensitive: license keys, profit figures and business plans.

46
00:04:25,640 --> 00:04:32,220
Now, social engineering. Phishing is a type of online scam, usually sent by email, SMS or phone call.

47
00:04:32,220 --> 00:04:39,060
Its purpose is to steal your credentials and sensitive information, or to install malware and ransomware.

48
00:04:39,060 --> 00:04:44,920
It tricks you into clicking a link and entering information, or downloading an attachment.

49
00:04:46,220 --> 00:04:51,450
Let's look at a phishing email. One: an urgent, alarming subject.

50
00:04:51,450 --> 00:04:56,770
Two: a fake email address — look, it ends in dot con, not dot com.

51
00:04:56,770 --> 00:05:00,960
Three: a phishing link that leads to a fake website.

52
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:11,270
So verify the sender, beware of urgency or threats, don't click suspicious links or attachments, and check for grammar mistakes.

53
00:05:11,270 --> 00:05:16,420
When you see these signs, it is about 80% likely to be phishing.

54
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:26,060
Email security means protecting email accounts, communication and data from unauthorized access, phishing, malware and scams.

55
00:05:26,060 --> 00:05:31,400
When a suspicious email arrives: do not click its links or open its attachments.

56
00:05:31,400 --> 00:05:38,340
Do not forward it to colleagues. Report it with the official reporting tool, to alert the security team.

57
00:05:38,340 --> 00:05:40,080
Then delete it completely.

58
00:05:41,380 --> 00:05:50,330
Some attachments are very high risk. Never open untrusted files ending in .exe, .bat, .vbs, .html or .scr.

59
00:05:50,330 --> 00:05:59,700
Word, Excel, PowerPoint, images and PDF files are lower risk — but still check the source before you open them.

60
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:05,480
Now let's make your Telegram account more secure and private.

61
00:06:05,480 --> 00:06:09,810
There are four key settings: turn on two-step verification.

62
00:06:09,810 --> 00:06:13,630
Check for suspicious devices and end those sessions.

63
00:06:13,630 --> 00:06:18,250
Turn off automatic file download. And turn on a local passcode.

64
00:06:18,250 --> 00:06:24,200
I'll show you each one step by step — the same settings are on your computer too.

65
00:06:25,500 --> 00:06:31,070
First, turn on two-step verification. Go to Settings and tap Privacy and Security.

66
00:06:31,070 --> 00:06:36,360
Tap Two-Step Verification. Type a new password, then type it again to confirm.

67
00:06:36,360 --> 00:06:43,090
Then add a recovery email — highly recommended — so you can get the password back if you forget it.

68
00:06:43,090 --> 00:06:47,500
Now a thief can't get into your account, even with your SMS code.

69
00:06:48,800 --> 00:06:55,120
Second, check for suspicious devices. In Settings, tap Devices to see all your sessions.

70
00:06:55,120 --> 00:07:00,590
Check carefully: a device or location you don't recognize is a warning sign.

71
00:07:00,590 --> 00:07:05,760
Tap Terminate All Other Sessions to end all other sessions, and confirm.

72
00:07:07,210 --> 00:07:09,830
Third, turn off automatic file download.

73
00:07:09,830 --> 00:07:12,950
Go to Settings and tap Data and Storage.

74
00:07:12,950 --> 00:07:19,270
Under Automatic Media Download, switch off automatic download for photos, videos and files.

75
00:07:19,270 --> 00:07:24,660
That way, unknown files are never saved to your device without you knowing.

76
00:07:26,110 --> 00:07:30,180
Fourth, turn on a local passcode — recommended for privacy.

77
00:07:30,180 --> 00:07:35,540
Go to Settings, then Privacy and Security, and tap Passcode and Face ID.

78
00:07:35,540 --> 00:07:39,130
Tap Turn Passcode On and type your passcode twice.

79
00:07:39,130 --> 00:07:45,040
Now, even if someone holds your phone, they can't open your Telegram.

80
00:07:46,490 --> 00:07:51,960
To sum up: 12+ character passwords, unique per account, with 2FA.

81
00:07:51,960 --> 00:07:57,180
HTTPS only, and no sensitive info on public sites.

82
00:07:57,180 --> 00:08:00,460
Lock your screen and secure documents when away.

83
00:08:00,460 --> 00:08:03,600
Keep devices updated, and report a loss immediately.

84
00:08:03,600 --> 00:08:07,380
Know your data: Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted.

85
00:08:07,380 --> 00:08:10,900
Pause before you click, verify, and report anything odd.

86
00:08:10,900 --> 00:08:15,790
Enable MFA, check sessions, and don't auto-trust attachments.

87
00:08:15,790 --> 00:08:20,220
And compliance protects client trust and our business.

88
00:08:21,520 --> 00:08:28,560
Now it's time to test your knowledge! Scan this QR code to take the quiz.

89
00:08:28,560 --> 00:08:29,520
Good luck!

90
00:08:30,820 --> 00:08:38,260
Thank you all for your attention! Remember: information security is everyone's responsibility.

91
00:08:38,260 --> 00:08:47,450
If you have any questions or need more clarification, contact the GRC team by the phone numbers or emails on screen.

92
00:08:47,450 --> 00:08:50,220
I'm the GRC team's robot — goodbye!
